spoint/mapspinner 'looking like tv8' is a FUNDAMENTAL fractal-scale conflict, not a bug: mapspinner samples the terrain fractal (broadShapeM in terrain.glsl, base freq 0.75 in UNIT-SPHERE space) by world-DIRECTION, so a feature's world-metre wavelength = (1/freq)*R. A SMALL radius (spoint 63600) therefore gives NARROW, SPIKY features (100x narrower than tv8's Earth-scale 6360000 broad mountains) + a single lowland biome at the anchor. You CANNOT have both a small planet AND tv8's broad varied terrain via radius/reliefScale alone. To decouple feature BREADTH from radius would require multiplying EVERY tuned frequency (broadShapeM octaves + the hand-derived analytic-derivative lit-normal broadShapeMD which must match exactly + massif/peak/canyon envelopes + the metre-based FD steps 150m/2400m) by a featureScale and keeping them congruent + the CPU height-cpu transpile - a large, regression-prone terrain-gen change (NOT a tail-of-session edit; risks breaking the lit-normal/carve/FD congruence). The user-facing answer: the editor TerrainPanel dials the trade-off (radius=feature breadth + planet size, reliefScale=relief height/visibility, octMax=detail-vs-smoothness); to look most like tv8, dial radius UP toward Earth-scale (accepting a bigger planet). reliefScale 0.01 was invisible-flat; ~0.05 shows relief but spiky at radius 63600. surfElev arg must be anchorHeight/radius (radius-fraction).